  "parts": [
    {
      "name": "Terminal bronchiole",
      "group": "Airways",
      "color": "#ddbbae",
      "description": "Terminal bronchiole: the last purely conducting airway (~0.5 mm). Simple cuboidal epithelium of ciliated cells and club cells, a complete coat of smooth muscle, and no cartilage or glands. Everything distal to it – the acinus – exchanges gas.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "airway",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.08,
        60.0,
        0.3,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Respiratory bronchioles",
      "group": "Airways",
      "color": "#ddbbae",
      "description": "Respiratory bronchioles: transitional airways whose cuboidal-epithelium wall is interrupted by scattered alveoli opening directly into the lumen – the first place gas exchange happens. There are 1–3 orders, each with more alveoli. Centriacinar emphysema of smokers destroys this region first.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "airway",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.08,
        60.0,
        0.3,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Bronchiolar smooth muscle",
      "group": "Airways",
      "color": "#bf786c",
      "description": "Bronchiolar smooth muscle: spiral bundles wrapping the terminal and respiratory bronchioles. With no cartilage to hold them open, bronchioles narrow when it contracts – bronchoconstriction in asthma and anaphylaxis, relieved by β2-agonists.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "muscle",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.08,
        70.0,
        0.35,
        1
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Pulmonary arteriole (deoxygenated)",
      "group": "Vessels",
      "color": "#4564ad",
      "description": "Pulmonary arteriole: carries deoxygenated blood and runs with the airway, branching as it branches (bronchovascular bundle) down to the alveolar ducts, where it breaks up into the capillaries. Hypoxic vasoconstriction diverts blood away from poorly ventilated alveoli; chronic hypoxia remodels it (pulmonary hypertension).  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "vein",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.06,
        80.0,
        0.25,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Pulmonary venule (oxygenated)",
      "group": "Vessels",
      "color": "#c34745",
      "description": "Pulmonary venule: carries oxygenated blood away from the capillaries. It runs at the periphery of the acinus and lobule, in the connective-tissue septa, well away from the airway and artery – so veins, not arteries, outline the lobules.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "artery",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.06,
        80.0,
        0.25,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Alveolar capillaries",
      "group": "Vessels",
      "color": "#ffffff",
      "description": "Alveolar capillaries: a network so dense that blood flows through the septa almost as a sheet. The air–blood barrier – type I cell, fused basement membranes and endothelium – is only ~0.5 µm thick. Continuous, non-fenestrated endothelium; blood arrives deoxygenated and leaves oxygenated. The broad network is schematically exposed in open wall channels so its anastomoses can be followed with tissue shown; microscopic embedding is explained separately in the representative barrier. Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "artery",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.06,
        80.0,
        0.25,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Interalveolar septa – alveolar ducts",
      "group": "Alveoli",
      "color": "#ddbbae",
      "description": "Alveolar ducts: long passages whose wall is nothing but the mouths of alveoli. The free edges of the septa ringing each mouth hold elastic, collagen and smooth muscle fibres (the knobs seen in section). The septa are thin sheets lined on both sides by type I pneumocytes (~95% of the surface, 0.1–0.2 µm thick) over a capillary network.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "connective",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.08,
        60.0,
        0.3,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Interalveolar septa – alveolar sacs",
      "group": "Alveoli",
      "color": "#ddbbae",
      "description": "Alveolar sacs: the blind ends of the alveolar ducts, a common space (atrium) surrounded by a cluster of alveoli. Each alveolus is ~200 µm across; there are ~300–500 million in the lungs, ~70 m² of surface. Panacinar emphysema (α1-antitrypsin deficiency) destroys the septa uniformly.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "connective",
      "label": false,
      "rank": 0.0,
      "clip": true,
      "bulk": false,
      "detail": [
        0.08,
        60.0,
        0.3,
        0
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Pores of Kohn",
      "group": "Alveoli",
      "color": "#ddbbae",
      "description": "Pores of Kohn: 10–15 µm holes through the interalveolar septa connecting neighbouring alveoli. They allow collateral ventilation past a blocked airway – and let bacteria and exudate spread in lobar pneumonia.  Review v2: representative specimen; original 1.2 mm/unit calibration retained. DRAFT AWAITING USER REVIEW.",
      "alpha": 1.0,
      "category": "connective",
